{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789910181,"nonce":"XtvFcLtbe5Ufus30evfJI4W/fPQ=","title":"Collector: a large allocation never triggers a collection","message":"A collection is only asked for from `alloc_slow`, on the pooled path, when a size class has nothing to reuse. `large_alloc` (anything over MAX_POOLED, 2 KB) charges the budget through `note_large` but never checks `wants_collection()`, so a program that churns large blocks while its small allocations recycle off the free lists collects once and then never again.\n\nReproducer: any Lua `s = s .. x` loop past 2 KB, or the zyntax_lua tokenize benchmark, whose `table.concat` over 300k parts is a fold of concatenations. `ZYNTAX_TRACE_GC=1` shows one collection at the 32 MB floor, then RSS climbs to 2.5 GB (5.8 GB on a variant) with no further collections. binary_trees and the other small-object kernels are unaffected: their pooled classes grow, so `alloc_slow` runs.\n\nFix: `large_alloc` collects when the budget is spent, before taking from libc, the same as `alloc_slow`. A pressure program that concatenates a large string every step guards it.","files":null}]}